---
Part IX, Chapter 5: The line scanner
Package: packages/tree-sitter-loom/
Language: C
---

# The line scanner

The line scanner is the classifier of Part III, written again in C for tree-sitter. Tree-sitter calls it at the start of every line, before the grammar's own lexer, and the scanner answers with the line's kind: a heading, an arrow, a tilde, a line of code, a fence around fenced code, a line of prose, and the rest. It reaches that answer the way `WeftClassifier` does. It keeps the same state, tests the same markers, and walks the same decision table in the same order, so a line that Loom reads as code is a line the editor colours as code.

The scanner consumes nothing for a line's kind. Each kind is a zero-width token that marks where the line starts, and the grammar then reads the line's marks and text itself. Only two of the scanner's tokens consume text: the line ending, and the name of a box, which no pattern of the grammar can find.

## The tokens it emits

The scanner's tokens follow the grammar's list of externals, entry for entry, because tree-sitter pairs the two lists by position. The first thirteen name a line's kind. A fence line is a frontmatter `---`, and a field line sits between the fences. A code fence line opens or closes fenced code. A verbatim line is inside a quote or opens one. A box line opens a box, a box body line sits inside it, and a box close line closes it. A divider line is a line of dashes that divides a chapter. The box name is the name on a line that opens a box. The newline ends a line, and the error sentinel tells the scanner when the parser is recovering from a syntax error.

=>

enum TokenType {
  FENCE_LINE,
  FIELD_LINE,
  HEADING_LINE,
  ARROW_LINE,
  TILDE_LINE,
  PROSE_LINE,
  CODE_LINE,
  CODE_FENCE_LINE,
  VERBATIM_LINE,
  BOX_LINE,
  BOX_BODY_LINE,
  BOX_CLOSE_LINE,
  DIVIDER_LINE,
  BOX_NAME,
  NEWLINE,
  ERROR_SENTINEL,
};

## What the scanner remembers

The scanner's state is the classifier's state. The classifier keeps the previous weft and reads a mode from it — preamble, code, or prose — so the scanner keeps the mode itself. The flags carry over one for one: whether a heading has been seen, whether the line sits in the frontmatter or a box, and whether the loom is a discussion. A quote and fenced code each keep the length and column of the fence that opened them, as the classifier keeps a `Fence`, and a length of zero means none is open. `awaiting_fence` is the classifier's wait for a fence after an arrow. `started` stands for the classifier's empty previous weft, the one moment a `---` may open the frontmatter.

Two values are the scanner's own. `at_line_start` records that the last token ended a line, so the next token names the next line's kind. It keeps the scanner from naming the same line twice, because a zero-width token leaves the parser where it was. `name_reach` remembers where a box's name ends, from the moment the scanner classifies the line that opens the box until it reads the name. It counts the characters from the end of the colons to the name's last character, and zero means no name waits to be read.

=>

enum Mode { PREAMBLE, CODE, PROSE };

typedef struct {
  bool at_line_start;
  bool started;
  bool seen_heading;
  bool in_frontmatter;
  uint8_t quote_length;
  uint8_t quote_column;
  bool in_box;
  bool discussion;
  uint8_t fenced_length;
  uint8_t fenced_column;
  bool awaiting_fence;
  uint8_t mode;
  uint32_t name_reach;
} Scanner;

## Reading a line ahead

The scanner must see a whole line to classify it. A `---` is a fence only when nothing but spaces follows it, and a box's name stops short of braces only when the braces end the line. Tree-sitter lets a scanner read past the end of the token it returns, so the scanner marks the token's end where the line begins and then reads on to the line's end.

`peek_line` gathers what the markers need into a `Line`. It keeps the line's first characters in `head`, enough for every marker at the start of a line. It records the line's length and the index of its last character that is not a space. A character outside ASCII is stored as a placeholder no marker matches, and `\s` in the classifier's patterns becomes `is_space`.

`peek_line` also follows the line's braces, the way the tokeniser's pattern `\{[^{}]*\}[ \t]*$` finds the braces that end a line. `brace_open` holds the last `{` that no other brace has followed yet. A `}` after it closes the pair, and `braces_close` records where. A `}` with no such `{` before it closes nothing, and `braces_close` drops back to -1. Beside them, `mark_before_open` and `mark_before_braces` keep the last character before each one's `{` that is not a space, so the scanner knows where a box's name ends when the braces end the line.

=>

#define HEAD_SIZE 48

typedef struct {
  char head[HEAD_SIZE];
  uint32_t head_length;
  uint32_t length;
  int32_t last_mark;
  int32_t brace_open;
  int32_t mark_before_open;
  int32_t braces_close;
  int32_t mark_before_braces;
} Line;

static bool is_space(int32_t c) {
  return c == ' ' || c == '\t' || c == '\v' || c == '\f' || c == '\r';
}

static void peek_line(TSLexer *lexer, Line *line) {
  memset(line, 0, sizeof(Line));
  line->last_mark = -1;
  line->brace_open = -1;
  line->braces_close = -1;
  while (!lexer->eof(lexer) && lexer->lookahead != '\n' &&
         lexer->lookahead != '\r') {
    int32_t c = lexer->lookahead;
    int32_t at = (int32_t)line->length;
    if (line->head_length < HEAD_SIZE)
      line->head[line->head_length++] = c < 128 ? (char)c : '\x01';
    if (c == '{') {
      line->brace_open = at;
      line->mark_before_open = line->last_mark;
    }
    if (c == '}') {
      line->braces_close = line->brace_open < 0 ? -1 : at;
      line->mark_before_braces = line->mark_before_open;
      line->brace_open = -1;
    }
    if (!is_space(c)) line->last_mark = at;
    line->length++;
    lexer->advance(lexer, false);
  }
}

## The markers a line can start with

Each marker test is one of the token patterns of Part II, read off the line's head. `head_has` tests for text at a position, `indent_of` measures the leading spaces, and `hashes_of` counts the leading `#` marks.

A heading is one to six `#` and a space, at the very start of the line. A frontmatter fence is `---` and nothing after it but spaces. An arrow is `=>` after any indent, and `is_bare_arrow` tells an arrow with nothing after it, the one that waits for a fence. A tilde stands at the start of the line, followed by a space or by the end of the line, so `~/bin` and a run of three tildes stay text. A divider is three or more dashes at the very start of the line and nothing after them but spaces.

=>

static bool head_has(const Line *line, uint32_t at, const char *text) {
  uint32_t n = (uint32_t)strlen(text);
  return at + n <= line->head_length && memcmp(line->head + at, text, n) == 0;
}

static uint32_t indent_of(const Line *line) {
  uint32_t i = 0;
  while (i < line->head_length && is_space(line->head[i])) i++;
  return i;
}

static uint32_t hashes_of(const Line *line) {
  uint32_t i = 0;
  while (i < line->head_length && line->head[i] == '#') i++;
  return i;
}

static bool is_heading(const Line *line) {
  uint32_t hashes = hashes_of(line);
  return hashes >= 1 && hashes <= 6 && head_has(line, hashes, " ");
}

static bool is_divider(const Line *line) {
  uint32_t dashes = 0;
  while (dashes < line->head_length && line->head[dashes] == '-') dashes++;
  return dashes >= 3 && line->last_mark == (int32_t)dashes - 1;
}

static bool is_frontmatter_fence(const Line *line) {
  return head_has(line, 0, "---") && line->last_mark == 2;
}

static bool is_arrow(const Line *line) {
  return head_has(line, indent_of(line), "=>");
}

static bool is_bare_arrow(const Line *line) {
  return is_arrow(line) && line->last_mark == (int32_t)indent_of(line) + 1;
}

static bool is_tilde(const Line *line) {
  return head_has(line, 0, "~") &&
         (line->length == 1 || is_space(line->head[1]));
}

~

The fences have markers of their own. `fence_of` reads a fence the way `openingFence` does: a run of three or more backticks after any indent, its length and its column. `closes_fence` tells whether a line closes an open fence, as `closesFence` does: a run at the same column, at least as long, with nothing after it. The scanner reads only the head of a line, so it counts a run of backticks up to the head's end, far past any fence a loom writes.

=>

static uint32_t fence_of(const Line *line, uint32_t *column) {
  uint32_t at = indent_of(line);
  uint32_t end = at;
  while (end < line->head_length && line->head[end] == '`') end++;
  *column = at;
  return end - at >= 3 ? end - at : 0;
}

static bool closes_fence(const Line *line, uint32_t length, uint32_t column) {
  uint32_t at;
  uint32_t found = fence_of(line, &at);
  return found >= length && at == column &&
         line->last_mark == (int32_t)(at + found) - 1;
}

~

A box line starts with a run of colons after any spaces or tabs, as the `BoxFence` pattern reads it. `box_fence_of` reads the run's length and column, and gives zero for fewer than three colons, which make no box line at all. Three colons open a box outside one, and `closes_box` tells the line that closes one: three colons with nothing after them but spaces.

`name_reach_of` measures the name on a line that opens a box. `name_end_of` finds the name's last character. When braces end the line, it is the last character before them that is not a space. Otherwise it is the line's own last character that is not a space. The reach counts the characters from the end of the colons up to that one. A line with no name, before its braces or at all, reaches nowhere.

=>

static uint32_t box_fence_of(const Line *line, uint32_t *column) {
  uint32_t at = 0;
  while (at < line->head_length &&
         (line->head[at] == ' ' || line->head[at] == '\t'))
    at++;
  uint32_t end = at;
  while (end < line->head_length && line->head[end] == ':') end++;
  *column = at;
  return end - at >= 3 ? end - at : 0;
}

static bool closes_box(const Line *line) {
  uint32_t column;
  return box_fence_of(line, &column) == 3 &&
         line->last_mark == (int32_t)column + 2;
}

static int32_t name_end_of(const Line *line) {
  bool braced =
      line->braces_close >= 0 && line->braces_close == line->last_mark;
  return braced ? line->mark_before_braces : line->last_mark;
}

static uint32_t name_reach_of(const Line *line, uint32_t column) {
  int32_t after = (int32_t)column + 3;
  int32_t end = name_end_of(line);
  return end < after ? 0 : (uint32_t)(end - after + 1);
}

~

A frontmatter field names a discussion the way the classifier's `namesDiscussion` reads it: the word `Discussion` after any indent, or after a part and a comma, as in `Part III, Discussion 2:`, then a colon, either straight after the word or after a number or a title. `names_discussion` reads that much off the line's head, which holds the start of any identity line a loom writes.

=>

static uint32_t skip_spaces(const Line *line, uint32_t at) {
  while (at < line->head_length && is_space(line->head[at])) at++;
  return at;
}

static uint32_t after_part(const Line *line, uint32_t at) {
  if (!head_has(line, at, "Part") || at + 4 >= line->head_length ||
      !is_space(line->head[at + 4]))
    return at;
  uint32_t name = skip_spaces(line, at + 4);
  uint32_t end = name;
  while (end < line->head_length && line->head[end] != ',' &&
         line->head[end] != ':')
    end++;
  if (end == name || end >= line->head_length || line->head[end] != ',')
    return at;
  return skip_spaces(line, end + 1);
}

static bool names_discussion(const Line *line) {
  uint32_t at = after_part(line, indent_of(line));
  if (!head_has(line, at, "Discussion")) return false;
  uint32_t end = at + 10;
  if (end < line->head_length && line->head[end] == ':') return true;
  if (end >= line->head_length || !is_space(line->head[end])) return false;
  while (end < line->head_length && line->head[end] != ':') end++;
  return end < line->head_length;
}

## Classifying one line

`classify_line` is the decision table of `probeWeft`, rule for rule. It returns the line's kind and leaves the state as the classifier's next state would be.

The frontmatter comes first: a `---` on the first line opens it, and every line through the closing `---` is a field. A field that names a discussion marks the loom as one, for every line that follows. A quote and a box come next. No line inside them opens anything until the quote or the box closes, and the mode follows what the classifier would record for such a line, prose after the first heading and preamble before it. Only the line `closes_box` accepts closes a box, so three colons with a name, or four colons, stay in its body, as the classifier keeps such a stray inside the box.

Every line starts with no box name to read. Only a line that opens a box gives `name_reach` a value.

=>

static enum TokenType classify_line(Scanner *s, const Line *line) {
  bool first = !s->started;
  s->started = true;
  s->name_reach = 0;

  if (first && is_frontmatter_fence(line)) {
    s->in_frontmatter = true;
    s->mode = PREAMBLE;
    return FENCE_LINE;
  }

  if (s->in_frontmatter) {
    s->mode = PREAMBLE;
    if (is_frontmatter_fence(line)) {
      s->in_frontmatter = false;
      return FENCE_LINE;
    }
    if (names_discussion(line)) s->discussion = true;
    return FIELD_LINE;
  }

  if (s->quote_length > 0) {
    if (closes_fence(line, s->quote_length, s->quote_column)) s->quote_length = 0;
    s->mode = s->seen_heading ? PROSE : PREAMBLE;
    return VERBATIM_LINE;
  }

  if (s->in_box) {
    s->mode = s->seen_heading ? PROSE : PREAMBLE;
    if (!closes_box(line)) return BOX_BODY_LINE;
    s->in_box = false;
    return BOX_CLOSE_LINE;
  }

  bool awaiting = s->awaiting_fence;
  s->awaiting_fence = false;

  ::[Fenced code and the headings]

  ::[The lines of a discussion]

  ::[The three modes]
}

## Fenced code and the headings

Fenced code outranks every rule after the inert regions. Inside it, the line that closes the fence is a code fence line that hands the next line to prose, and every other line is code, a `#` line included. A waiting arrow comes next: a fence on the first line after it that is not blank opens fenced code. A heading comes next, and it opens a section. Every line before the first heading is preamble. A divider comes last of these. Past the first heading, a line of dashes ends code or prose, as a heading does, and hands the next line to the preamble of its division.

=>

if (s->fenced_length > 0) {
  if (closes_fence(line, s->fenced_length, s->fenced_column)) {
    s->fenced_length = 0;
    s->mode = PROSE;
    return CODE_FENCE_LINE;
  }
  s->mode = CODE;
  return CODE_LINE;
}

if (awaiting) {
  uint32_t column;
  uint32_t length = fence_of(line, &column);
  if (length > 0) {
    s->fenced_length = (uint8_t)(length > 255 ? 255 : length);
    s->fenced_column = (uint8_t)(column > 255 ? 255 : column);
    s->mode = CODE;
    return CODE_FENCE_LINE;
  }
}

if (is_heading(line)) {
  s->mode = PREAMBLE;
  s->seen_heading = true;
  return HEADING_LINE;
}

if (!s->seen_heading) return prose_kind(s, line, PROSE_LINE, PREAMBLE);

if (is_divider(line)) {
  s->mode = PREAMBLE;
  return DIVIDER_LINE;
}

## The lines of a discussion

A discussion holds no code, as the chapter "Discussions" describes, so the scanner reads its lines by the rules the classifier keeps for one, once the heading and the divider have had their turn. A tilde opens a message wherever it stands, since there is no code for it to close, so a tilde line is a tilde line in every mode, and the lines after it are prose. An arrow opens nothing, so its line is prose. Every line that is not a tilde line is prose, or preamble before the first message, and it may still open a quote or a box, as any line of prose may.

=>

if (s->discussion) {
  if (is_tilde(line)) return prose_kind(s, line, TILDE_LINE, PROSE);
  return prose_kind(s, line, PROSE_LINE, s->mode == PREAMBLE ? PREAMBLE : PROSE);
}

## The three modes

After the first heading the mode decides. In the preamble, an arrow opens code and a tilde opens prose. In code, a tilde returns to prose and every other line stays code. In prose, an arrow re-enters code and every other line stays prose. An arrow with nothing after it starts the wait for a fence, and a blank line of code keeps it.

=>

switch (s->mode) {
  case PREAMBLE:
    if (is_arrow(line)) {
      s->awaiting_fence = is_bare_arrow(line);
      s->mode = CODE;
      return ARROW_LINE;
    }
    if (is_tilde(line)) return prose_kind(s, line, TILDE_LINE, PROSE);
    return prose_kind(s, line, PROSE_LINE, PREAMBLE);
  case CODE:
    if (is_tilde(line)) return prose_kind(s, line, TILDE_LINE, PROSE);
    s->awaiting_fence = awaiting && line->last_mark < 0;
    return CODE_LINE;
  default:
    if (is_arrow(line)) {
      s->awaiting_fence = is_bare_arrow(line);
      s->mode = CODE;
      return ARROW_LINE;
    }
    return prose_kind(s, line, PROSE_LINE, PROSE);
}

## Where a quote or a box opens

Only a line of prose opens a quote or a box, as in the classifier, so a fence or a run of colons inside code is left to the code's own language. `prose_kind` takes the kind a prose line would have and settles the regions it may open. A line that opens a quote is verbatim, since the tokeniser skips the anchors on a quote's first line as well. Three colons open a box. The line is a box line, and `name_reach` keeps its name for the scan to read. Four or more colons open nothing. The tokeniser faults such a stray and reads no anchor on it, so the line is verbatim too. Any other prose line keeps its kind.

=>

static enum TokenType prose_kind(Scanner *s, const Line *line,
                                 enum TokenType kind, enum Mode mode) {
  uint32_t column;
  uint32_t length = fence_of(line, &column);
  s->mode = mode;
  s->quote_length = (uint8_t)(length > 255 ? 255 : length);
  s->quote_column = (uint8_t)(column > 255 ? 255 : column);
  if (length > 0) return VERBATIM_LINE;
  uint32_t colons = box_fence_of(line, &column);
  if (colons == 0) return kind;
  if (colons > 3) return VERBATIM_LINE;
  s->in_box = true;
  s->name_reach = name_reach_of(line, column);
  return BOX_LINE;
}

## Ending a line

A line ends at a line feed, a carriage return and line feed, or a lone carriage return. The scanner consumes whichever it finds and marks the next line's start. A file's last line may have no ending at all, and the scanner then ends it with an empty token at the end of the file.

=>

static bool scan_newline(Scanner *s, TSLexer *lexer) {
  if (lexer->lookahead == '\r') lexer->advance(lexer, false);
  if (lexer->lookahead == '\n') lexer->advance(lexer, false);
  lexer->mark_end(lexer);
  s->at_line_start = true;
  lexer->result_symbol = NEWLINE;
  return true;
}

## Reading a box's name

The grammar asks for a box's name right after the colons that open the box, and `scan_box_name` reads it there. It skips the spaces before the name, so they stay out of the name's node, and then reads as far as `name_reach` says. The reach is spent once read, so the scanner never reads one name twice.

=>

static bool scan_box_name(Scanner *s, TSLexer *lexer) {
  uint32_t reach = s->name_reach;
  s->name_reach = 0;
  while (reach > 0 && is_space(lexer->lookahead)) {
    lexer->advance(lexer, true);
    reach--;
  }
  while (reach > 0) {
    lexer->advance(lexer, false);
    reach--;
  }
  lexer->mark_end(lexer);
  lexer->result_symbol = BOX_NAME;
  return true;
}

## The scan

Tree-sitter calls `scan` with the list of tokens the parser can accept at that point. Inside a line, the scanner answers at the line's end, and at the one place a box's name may stand, and it leaves the rest of the text to the grammar. At a line's start, it reads the line, classifies it, and names the line's kind, provided the parser can accept that kind there. While the parser recovers from an error it accepts anything. The scanner then names a line's kind regardless, so its state keeps pace with the lines even through broken text, but it reads no box name, since it cannot tell where on the line the parser stands. A file's end after its last line ending holds no line, so the scanner names none there.

=>

static bool scan(Scanner *s, TSLexer *lexer, const bool *valid) {
  bool recovering = valid[ERROR_SENTINEL];

  if (!s->at_line_start) {
    if (valid[BOX_NAME] && !recovering && s->name_reach > 0)
      return scan_box_name(s, lexer);
    if (!valid[NEWLINE] && !recovering) return false;
    if (lexer->eof(lexer) || lexer->lookahead == '\n' ||
        lexer->lookahead == '\r')
      return scan_newline(s, lexer);
    return false;
  }

  if (lexer->eof(lexer)) return false;

  lexer->mark_end(lexer);
  Line line;
  peek_line(lexer, &line);
  enum TokenType kind = classify_line(s, &line);
  if (!valid[kind] && !recovering) return false;
  s->at_line_start = false;
  lexer->result_symbol = kind;
  return true;
}

## Saving and restoring the state

Tree-sitter reparses only the part of a file an edit touched. To start in the middle, it saves the scanner's state after every token the scanner returns and restores it before the next call. Twelve of the state's values are small, so each takes one byte, and the reach of a box's name takes the four bytes after them. A fresh scanner, or one restored from nothing, stands at the start of a line with every flag clear.

=>

void *tree_sitter_loom_external_scanner_create(void) {
  Scanner *s = ts_calloc(1, sizeof(Scanner));
  s->at_line_start = true;
  return s;
}

void tree_sitter_loom_external_scanner_destroy(void *payload) {
  ts_free(payload);
}

unsigned tree_sitter_loom_external_scanner_serialize(void *payload,
                                                     char *buffer) {
  Scanner *s = payload;
  buffer[0] = (char)s->at_line_start;
  buffer[1] = (char)s->started;
  buffer[2] = (char)s->seen_heading;
  buffer[3] = (char)s->in_frontmatter;
  buffer[4] = (char)s->quote_length;
  buffer[5] = (char)s->quote_column;
  buffer[6] = (char)s->in_box;
  buffer[7] = (char)s->fenced_length;
  buffer[8] = (char)s->fenced_column;
  buffer[9] = (char)s->awaiting_fence;
  buffer[10] = (char)s->mode;
  buffer[11] = (char)s->discussion;
  memcpy(buffer + 12, &s->name_reach, sizeof(s->name_reach));
  return 16;
}

void tree_sitter_loom_external_scanner_deserialize(void *payload,
                                                   const char *buffer,
                                                   unsigned length) {
  Scanner *s = payload;
  memset(s, 0, sizeof(Scanner));
  s->at_line_start = true;
  if (length < 16) return;
  s->at_line_start = buffer[0];
  s->started = buffer[1];
  s->seen_heading = buffer[2];
  s->in_frontmatter = buffer[3];
  s->quote_length = (uint8_t)buffer[4];
  s->quote_column = (uint8_t)buffer[5];
  s->in_box = buffer[6];
  s->fenced_length = (uint8_t)buffer[7];
  s->fenced_column = (uint8_t)buffer[8];
  s->awaiting_fence = buffer[9];
  s->mode = (uint8_t)buffer[10];
  s->discussion = buffer[11];
  memcpy(&s->name_reach, buffer + 12, sizeof(s->name_reach));
}

bool tree_sitter_loom_external_scanner_scan(void *payload, TSLexer *lexer,
                                            const bool *valid_symbols) {
  return scan(payload, lexer, valid_symbols);
}

## The scanner module [src/scanner.c]

Tree-sitter compiles `src/scanner.c` beside the parser it generates. The scanner takes the lexer's interface from tree-sitter's parser header and the allocator a grammar should use from its allocation header.

=>

#include "tree_sitter/alloc.h"
#include "tree_sitter/parser.h"

